From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VIctory contributor <contributor@users.noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:11:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/43] Textures picked by index: the recheck's fingerprint
 reads every mip of every slice

The recheck added in a803c87 compares a fingerprint of 16 KiB at the
start, middle and end of a texture's memory. Each slice stores its mips
smallest first with mip 0 last, so for a plain 2D texture those windows
land on the tail and mip 0 only: mips 1 and 2 (about a quarter of the
bytes) and every mip in between are never read. In an array or cube map
they see two slices of six or more. A mip or slice that arrived after the
upload there was still missed (the commit's own "known gap").

Layout::sample_ranges (victory-formats) gives one range at the start of
each mip of each slice, tail mips at their places in the tail block: 1 KiB
each, at most 64 per texture, spread evenly over slices and mips when
there are more, adjacent ranges joined into one read. The ranges are
worked out from the staging buffer's layout at upload, kept with the slot,
and read on top of the three windows, so the new fingerprint sees every
change the old one saw. A 4096-square texture with all its mips: 10 reads,
12 KiB more per look; a 512 cube map with 10 mips: 40 reads, 52 KiB.

VICTORY_HEAP_RECHECK_MIPS=0 keeps the three windows alone (the
fingerprint is then the same as before).

Checked: victory-formats tests on Linux, including a test that every mip
of every slice (tail mips too) has a range over bytes its elements are
stored at, found through Layout::offset rather than the mip offsets; the
Windows target builds and passes clippy with and without diagnostics.
Not yet run in the game.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 6f1fc6b53f75990d7b0708df6167e1cef7958220)
